sequenceDiagram
  %% Proposed design (2026-11-07, for Nick's review, nothing built): step 1 of the task VM egress allowlist. It records, log only, which outside hosts a task VM connects to during real task work, per VM and per task group. Nothing is blocked. Step 2 derives the allowlist from this data and enforces it through a proxy outside the VM. The page: docs/reference/task-vm-egress-log.md.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: task VM egress log
  %% kind: task_vm.egress_log
  autonumber
  %% lane VM icon=server pill=task VM tone=purple
  %% lane BR icon=server pill=task VM host tone=amber
  %% lane AG icon=server pill=observer tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  participant VM as Task VM<br/>and its containers
  participant BR as Host bridge<br/>orbittask0
  participant AG as orbit-agent<br/>on the host
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>Ansible phase
  SW->>BR: Turn on the logs<br/>once per host · a part
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one idempotent part of the host phase (`swamp/ansible/task-vm-host.yml`, tag `egress-log`): `raw.dnsmasq` on the bridge (`log-queries=extra`, a log file), an `nftables` table `orbit_egress` with a logging rule, an rsyslog rule that splits its lines into a file, and a logrotate file
  %%> - applied in a quiet window: a dnsmasq reload blinks DNS and DHCP of running VMs for a second
  VM->>BR: Ask for a name<br/>DNS to the bridge address
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the VM's resolver, and the DNS of its nested containers behind the VM's own NAT, all ask the bridge's dnsmasq
  %%> - dnsmasq logs each query and its answers with a serial number, so a name pairs exactly with the addresses it returned
  %%> What it misses:
  %%> - a fixed IP, DNS over HTTPS, a resolver other than the bridge; the connection log below sees all three
  VM->>BR: Open a connection<br/>forwarded and NAT'd
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the kernel forwards the first packet of the flow and the `orbit_egress` rule logs it with the prefix `orbit-egress `: the VM's address, the outside address, port and protocol
  %%> - a dynamic set with a ten-minute timeout logs each distinct source, destination, protocol and port once, so a busy install writes a few hundred lines
  %%> What it misses:
  %%> - traffic inside the WireGuard tunnel: it holds only private Orbit addresses, the host sees one UDP flow to the hub
  AG->>BR: Read the records<br/>two logs · leases
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - every 5 s the agent reads the new lines of `/var/log/incus/dnsmasq.<network>.log` and `/var/log/orbit/task-egress.log`, and the bridge's leases and hosts files
  %%> - the VM is named by its MAC address: the lease gives MAC to IP, and the host's Incus config gives MAC to the VM name (`beast-tvm-3`); nothing the VM says about itself is trusted
  AG->>AG: Roll up per VM<br/>one row per host and port
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - a connection to an address that the same VM resolved joins that name (name, port, protocol, first seen, sources `dns` and `conn`)
  %%> - an address with no name stays an address (sources `conn` only); a connection to a public resolver on 443 or 853 is marked `doh`
  %%> - a row that has not changed adds nothing, so the fact stays small and a quiet VM sends nothing
  AG-->>GW: Send the egress fact<br/>on change · within 15 s
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one `egress` fact per VM name on the host Node, over the existing `POST /api/v1/agent/facts`; the agent sends nothing else and opens no port
  GW->>GW: Attribute to the group<br/>VM name and time
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the Gateway holds the claim of each VM (VM name, group, start and end) and adds each row to the group that held the VM when the row was first seen
  %%> - rows go to `task_group_egress`, unique per group, host or address, port and protocol; the group keeps them after its VM and Node are deleted
  Note over GW: Read it: a table per group<br/>and a union per Project
  %%> Result:
  %%> - `orbit tasks:egress <group>` lists the outside hosts of one group; with `--project` it lists the union over the Project's groups, split by feature work and proofs
  %%> - step 2 turns that union into the allowlist
